WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:07.212
Cloe Shasha Brooks: Salehe,
thank you so much for joining us.

00:00:07.212 --> 00:00:09.005
Salehe Bembury: Thanks for having me.

00:00:09.005 --> 00:00:12.676
CSB: Among many of your popular designs,
you made Crocs cool.

00:00:12.676 --> 00:00:17.013
And this is called the Crocs Pollex clog,

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:18.765
based on your own fingerprint mold.

00:00:18.765 --> 00:00:21.226
And what I love is
that Crocs reach out to you

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:23.228
asking if they could send you some shoes.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.981
And you said, "How about
we actually collaborate instead?"

00:00:26.022 --> 00:00:27.232
And they said yes.

00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:30.360
So once they said yes, how did you
decide to do this specifically?

00:00:30.402 --> 00:00:33.530
SB: It was a great honor
because they first wanted to collaborate.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.909
And then when I sent them
what I didn’t know was a kid’s Croc shoe,

00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:39.578
they let me know that they loved

00:00:39.578 --> 00:00:41.663
that I was thinking
of the form of the Croc,

00:00:41.663 --> 00:00:45.125
which a lot of collaborators in the past
were simply reskinning the Croc,

00:00:45.166 --> 00:00:46.376
maybe adding jibbitz.

00:00:46.418 --> 00:00:47.961
And I was thinking of the form,

00:00:47.961 --> 00:00:50.422
and that comes from my
industrial-design background.

00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:53.300
Once they saw that I was interested
in manipulating the form,

00:00:53.341 --> 00:00:56.344
they asked me if I wanted to be
the first collaborative designer

00:00:56.386 --> 00:00:58.847
to create a Croc from scratch,

00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:02.309
which I immediately did
a Birdman hand rub and said yes.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:03.685
(Laughter)

00:01:03.727 --> 00:01:05.979
And so this is the result of that.

00:01:06.021 --> 00:01:09.774
Initially I had to make sure
that I fostered and maintained

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:12.277
the existing elements of the Croc DNA

00:01:12.277 --> 00:01:15.488
and made sure that I didn't
scare away the existing consumer

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:18.241
and getting the honor of working
with a brand like Crocs,

00:01:18.241 --> 00:01:20.619
you get to work with their
heritage and their DNA.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:22.454
So I had to make sure to maintain that

00:01:22.495 --> 00:01:25.165
and then also introduce
my own brand identity,

00:01:25.206 --> 00:01:26.499
put it through a new lens,

00:01:26.541 --> 00:01:29.044
and potentially speak to a new audience.

00:01:29.044 --> 00:01:30.670
CSB: You know, it's pretty amazing

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.840
because you've had a really
amazing journey as a footwear designer.

00:01:33.882 --> 00:01:35.925
You started at Payless in 2009,

00:01:35.967 --> 00:01:38.887
and then you started doing
collaborations with a lot of brands

00:01:38.887 --> 00:01:42.057
like New Balance, Cole Haan,
Versace, to name a few.

00:01:42.057 --> 00:01:44.392
And now you run your own
brand called Spunge,

00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:45.560
that's what those are.

00:01:45.602 --> 00:01:48.605
So to help paint the picture
of how you've forged your own path,

00:01:48.647 --> 00:01:51.149
I'd love for you to share
the story that I just love,

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:52.651
of you being on your couch,

00:01:52.692 --> 00:01:55.070
eating cereal in a moment of unemployment

00:01:55.111 --> 00:01:57.989
and you somehow went
from scrolling on LinkedIn

00:01:58.031 --> 00:02:00.700
to convincing Versace
to give you your dream job.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.077
Will you tell us that story?

00:02:02.077 --> 00:02:03.828
SB: So, cereal on my couch,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.581
that is a description
of the image of unemployment

00:02:06.623 --> 00:02:08.917
that I was experiencing at that time.

00:02:08.958 --> 00:02:12.921
I was using all of the tools
that have been given to us for outreach,

00:02:12.921 --> 00:02:15.382
LinkedIn, Craigslist,

00:02:15.382 --> 00:02:17.258
maybe it was even Facebook at the time,

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.011
and sending messages to whoever
would accept them.

00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:22.013
I reached out to a
Versace design director,

00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:23.473
and I proposed an opportunity.

00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:26.434
I said, the sneaker industry
is a multibillion-dollar business,

00:02:26.476 --> 00:02:29.979
and traditionally, that consumer
isn't paying attention to fashion brands

00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:32.565
or traditionally paying attention
to the sneaker brands

00:02:32.607 --> 00:02:35.151
that we're all used to
and probably wearing right now.

00:02:35.151 --> 00:02:37.612
But then there was this
mass education that happened

00:02:37.612 --> 00:02:39.948
that Raf Simons, Adidas,
Nike, Comme des Garçons,

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:42.409
and now this massive consumer
was aware of the fact

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:44.327
that fashion houses can make sneakers.

00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:45.745
And so I reached out to them,

00:02:45.787 --> 00:02:48.248
a few days later,
Donatella herself responded.

00:02:48.289 --> 00:02:49.999
She said she loved my ideas.

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:53.211
Perfect timing for that photo.

00:02:53.211 --> 00:02:56.589
She said that she wanted me
to come out to Milan

00:02:56.589 --> 00:03:00.385
and present to her what I thought
the sneaker program could be.

00:03:00.385 --> 00:03:02.595
I went out to Milan a few days later,

00:03:02.637 --> 00:03:06.975
presented a combination of merchandising
and design and sales insight,

00:03:07.016 --> 00:03:08.351
and she loved it.

00:03:08.351 --> 00:03:10.186
And she was like, "I want to hire you.

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:12.063
I want you to move to Milan right away.

00:03:12.063 --> 00:03:13.273
Do you want to consult?"

00:03:13.314 --> 00:03:15.942
She was really open about what
the opportunity could be.

00:03:15.942 --> 00:03:17.110
Because she was so open,

00:03:17.152 --> 00:03:20.029
I asked if I could have
my own design studio in Los Angeles

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:22.657
and travel to Milan monthly
for factory visits and shows

00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:23.867
and she said yes.

00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:26.786
So I went outside and did my
pursuit of happiness --

00:03:26.786 --> 00:03:28.288
cry in the streets --

00:03:28.329 --> 00:03:30.248
because my life changed instantly.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.793
But really what that was more
about was the power of asking

00:03:33.793 --> 00:03:35.503
and what that can bring you.

00:03:35.503 --> 00:03:40.550
(Applause)

00:03:40.592 --> 00:03:42.802
CSB: So throughout your career,

00:03:42.802 --> 00:03:45.472
you've always used color
and form and function

00:03:45.513 --> 00:03:47.307
in a way that's felt really playful.

00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:49.601
There's so many nuanced details
in your designs,

00:03:49.601 --> 00:03:52.228
like there's so many Easter eggs
that people could find

00:03:52.228 --> 00:03:53.855
if they know what to look for.

00:03:53.897 --> 00:03:56.191
So I'm going to show
some pictures of your shoes,

00:03:56.191 --> 00:03:59.861
and I'd love to hear you share
your design philosophy for each of them.

00:03:59.861 --> 00:04:01.070
Tell us about these.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.072
SB: So this was
my first-ever collaboration

00:04:03.114 --> 00:04:05.241
with a Chinese brand called Anta.

00:04:05.742 --> 00:04:08.036
This was a from-scratch collaboration.

00:04:08.077 --> 00:04:10.997
A lot of collaborations these days
are color-ups and skinings,

00:04:11.039 --> 00:04:14.667
but this was, I guess the result
of a white piece of paper.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:17.045
And I guess what I was trying
to accomplish here

00:04:17.086 --> 00:04:20.048
was an introduction to footwear,

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:22.217
the footwear industry
and the audience --

00:04:22.258 --> 00:04:24.803
because before that I was working
at different brands,

00:04:24.803 --> 00:04:27.263
and this was the first time
that a piece of footwear

00:04:27.305 --> 00:04:28.723
was going to feature my name.

00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:32.352
So there was a lot of things that I had
to introduce to my existing audience.

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:33.686
CSB: How about this one?

00:04:33.686 --> 00:04:34.938
SB: This is the HALI 1.

00:04:34.938 --> 00:04:37.774
It's the first signature sneaker
of Tyrese Haliburton.

00:04:37.774 --> 00:04:40.193
I became a footwear
designer because of basketball.

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:43.863
That was my kind of initial inspiration
that made me feel the emotions

00:04:43.863 --> 00:04:45.907
that I believe I'm giving to others now.

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:47.242
It is with Puma.

00:04:47.617 --> 00:04:51.079
He recently wore it
in the previous NBA finals.

00:04:51.746 --> 00:04:53.331
It had a pretty tragic ending,

00:04:53.373 --> 00:04:58.211
but it was still amazing
to be a part of that shoe

00:04:58.253 --> 00:05:02.006
and for it to fall at that part
of my career was really special.

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:03.258
CSB: I love these.

00:05:03.258 --> 00:05:05.718
SB: This was a shoe I did with Pyer Moss,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.762
which is a high-fashion brand.

00:05:07.804 --> 00:05:11.266
The creative director reached out to me,
and he asked me to create a shoe

00:05:11.266 --> 00:05:14.102
that represented the feeling
of being financially stuck.

00:05:14.102 --> 00:05:16.062
I knew that I couldn't use concrete,

00:05:16.104 --> 00:05:20.316
so then I quickly thought of using
silicone as the solution.

00:05:20.316 --> 00:05:21.818
And that's what I made.

00:05:21.818 --> 00:05:23.820
CSB: And that was in a runway show, right?

00:05:23.862 --> 00:05:28.032
SB: Yes, this was in like
a SW ’16 runway show, or SS.

00:05:28.074 --> 00:05:29.242
CSB: How about these?

00:05:29.284 --> 00:05:33.371
SB: These are a bunch of images
from my Moncler Genius collaboration.

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:35.415
This was my first
ready-to-wear collection.

00:05:35.415 --> 00:05:39.002
And as of late, a lot of my work
has been described as like world-building.

00:05:39.043 --> 00:05:41.045
And this was that in real form,

00:05:41.087 --> 00:05:43.339
because I got to create
a full installation

00:05:43.381 --> 00:05:47.594
that helped further tell the story
of the ready-to-wear collection

00:05:47.594 --> 00:05:49.554
and featured footwear, accessories,

00:05:49.554 --> 00:05:52.432
and there were some pretty
big billboards as well,

00:05:52.473 --> 00:05:54.767
which I hadn't experienced
thus far in my career.

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:57.228
CSB: And when you did
this ready-to-wear collection,

00:05:57.228 --> 00:06:00.023
how did you go from just doing
footwear to clothing?

00:06:00.064 --> 00:06:01.816
Like, how did you think about that?

00:06:01.816 --> 00:06:03.902
SB: I think one of the most
difficult things

00:06:03.902 --> 00:06:06.988
for an independent designer
is creating your signature language.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.490
Like, what is that signature identity

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.493
that someone can recognize
without even seeing a logo?

00:06:12.535 --> 00:06:16.456
So once I kind of established that,
I think it now makes it easy

00:06:16.456 --> 00:06:20.084
to bring that across
different categories of product.

00:06:20.793 --> 00:06:23.796
CSB: And then this video
is just so charming.

00:06:23.838 --> 00:06:25.757
SB: That is Thundercat, that's Steven.

00:06:26.299 --> 00:06:30.178
That was for a New Balance
project called "Sand be the Time."

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.931
A lot of the projects
are influenced by experiences.

00:06:32.931 --> 00:06:35.183
So this was from a trip --

00:06:35.642 --> 00:06:36.809
Where was that?

00:06:36.809 --> 00:06:38.603
That was the pink sand dunes in Utah.

00:06:38.645 --> 00:06:42.106
And that basically was the foundation
of the story for that shoe.

00:06:42.148 --> 00:06:45.777
So the average person looking
at that shoe might see a pink shoe.

00:06:45.818 --> 00:06:48.071
But then with the story
of the pink sand dunes

00:06:48.112 --> 00:06:54.077
and relating the execution of the chenille
into the colors of the sunset.

00:06:54.077 --> 00:06:55.745
Now you’ve cultivated a community,

00:06:55.745 --> 00:06:57.914
you’ve invited people
to come into your world

00:06:57.956 --> 00:06:59.123
and you've told a story.

00:06:59.123 --> 00:07:01.250
CSB: You got really into hiking in nature.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.419
You started designing
outdoor shoes and gear,

00:07:03.461 --> 00:07:06.631
and in one instance you translated
Arizona's Antelope Canyon

00:07:06.673 --> 00:07:10.385
into a shoe called Peace Be the Journey,
which came out in 2020.

00:07:10.385 --> 00:07:12.929
And that shoe, even though
it came out six years ago,

00:07:12.929 --> 00:07:16.140
is still so popular that it's being
sold for markup

00:07:16.182 --> 00:07:19.018
on resale sites like eBay
like, to this day,

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.187
consistently, for a lot of money.

00:07:21.229 --> 00:07:24.357
So tell us why you think
this shoe is still so popular.

00:07:25.566 --> 00:07:28.778
SB: Potentially that I told a story
that people could resonate with,

00:07:28.778 --> 00:07:31.781
this was influenced by a trip
to Antelope Canyon,

00:07:31.781 --> 00:07:34.033
a beautiful place,
for those that haven't been.

00:07:34.033 --> 00:07:38.538
And ultimately the goal was to make
this shoe represent that experience.

00:07:38.579 --> 00:07:41.249
So if you're looking at that shoe,
it's an orange shoe.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:44.210
But then when I tie the orange
to the tones of Antelope Canyon

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:46.295
and the blue of the N represents the sky,

00:07:46.295 --> 00:07:49.924
and the maroons represent
the tones in the sunset.

00:07:50.258 --> 00:07:54.262
And then the campaign
tells that story further.

00:07:54.262 --> 00:07:55.847
Now when people wear that shoe,

00:07:55.888 --> 00:07:58.307
sure, it may be a shoe
that they think looks cool,

00:07:58.349 --> 00:08:01.102
but then they now feel like
they're a part of a community.

00:08:01.102 --> 00:08:03.938
I remember when I first started
traveling to China for work,

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:06.274
I would always look
for people wearing Supreme

00:08:06.274 --> 00:08:08.735
because I assumed
that there was a higher likelihood

00:08:08.776 --> 00:08:09.944
that they spoke English.

00:08:09.944 --> 00:08:12.822
So sometimes there are visual
cues that signify a community.

00:08:12.864 --> 00:08:14.032
CSB: It's so cool.

00:08:14.032 --> 00:08:15.825
So staying on that New Balance theme,

00:08:15.867 --> 00:08:19.495
you designed the New Balance
574 Yurt shoe,

00:08:19.495 --> 00:08:20.955
with a whistle on the heel.

00:08:20.997 --> 00:08:22.165
SB: Yes.

00:08:22.206 --> 00:08:24.500
CSB: So most designers
would never think of that.

00:08:24.542 --> 00:08:28.254
I'm going to share this with all of you
so you can see what I'm talking about.

00:08:32.383 --> 00:08:33.509
(Whistling)

00:08:33.551 --> 00:08:35.511
(Laughter)

00:08:39.057 --> 00:08:40.850
(Laughter)

00:08:40.892 --> 00:08:42.435
CSB: So how'd you think of that?

00:08:43.144 --> 00:08:44.812
SB: So New Balance tasked me

00:08:44.812 --> 00:08:48.316
with the opportunity of creating
a shoe from scratch.

00:08:48.983 --> 00:08:51.069
Around this time, my buddy Samuel Ross,

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:52.945
who's a designer based out of London,

00:08:52.945 --> 00:08:55.406
had this Nike shoe
called the “Zoom Vomero,”

00:08:55.448 --> 00:08:58.451
and it featured a molded plastic piece
on the back of the shoe.

00:08:58.451 --> 00:08:59.744
And I'd wear them a lot.

00:08:59.786 --> 00:09:02.747
And people would always ask me,
what does that molded piece do?

00:09:02.789 --> 00:09:04.540
And I would respond, nothing at all.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.334
And people seemed to still accept it.

00:09:06.334 --> 00:09:08.044
And I found that really fascinating

00:09:08.044 --> 00:09:10.922
because it was a very unused area
of real estate of the shoe.

00:09:10.922 --> 00:09:13.758
And so I thought to myself,
what if this area of real estate

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.676
could be used for something functional?

00:09:15.676 --> 00:09:18.638
Obviously, if that functional thing
was filled with technology,

00:09:18.679 --> 00:09:20.681
now you’re looking at a $2,000 shoe,

00:09:20.681 --> 00:09:21.933
so that wasn't an option.

00:09:21.933 --> 00:09:24.936
Then I thought of how I could
manipulate plastic to function.

00:09:24.977 --> 00:09:26.312
That became a whistle.

00:09:26.312 --> 00:09:29.482
Truthfully, I created the whistle
without thinking of the why,

00:09:29.524 --> 00:09:30.775
the purpose,

00:09:30.775 --> 00:09:33.111
I just thought a whistle was cool.

00:09:33.152 --> 00:09:37.365
And then, for the prototype,
I glued a whistle into a New Balance shoe,

00:09:37.406 --> 00:09:39.951
and I met with the team
and I told them a whole story

00:09:39.951 --> 00:09:42.161
of think about camping with your friends,

00:09:42.203 --> 00:09:43.663
and it's almost dusk

00:09:43.704 --> 00:09:46.124
and you stop to take
a picture of some deer

00:09:46.165 --> 00:09:47.750
and your friends walk ahead.

00:09:47.792 --> 00:09:49.919
And now you've lost them.

00:09:49.961 --> 00:09:52.296
And then I grabbed a shoe
from beneath the table,

00:09:52.338 --> 00:09:55.174
blew the whistle
and then they all slow-clapped.

00:09:55.216 --> 00:09:56.801
(Laughter)

00:09:56.843 --> 00:10:00.263
And so again, that was

00:10:00.263 --> 00:10:03.307
maybe a little bit of my industrial
design background, you know,

00:10:03.307 --> 00:10:05.268
trying to figure out how to find utility

00:10:05.309 --> 00:10:07.603
within maybe something
that doesn't have as much.

00:10:07.603 --> 00:10:09.522
But then more importantly,

00:10:09.522 --> 00:10:12.650
I think it's kind of cool to just
do something first in footwear,

00:10:12.692 --> 00:10:14.569
there's never been a whistle on a shoe.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:17.446
And if you were to think
about how much energy it would take

00:10:17.446 --> 00:10:20.199
for you to scream if you broke
your ankle on a hike alone,

00:10:20.241 --> 00:10:23.286
compared to the amount of energy
that would just simply take you

00:10:23.327 --> 00:10:24.495
to blow a whistle.

00:10:24.537 --> 00:10:27.957
So, you know, giving the consumer
a tool seemed like a good opportunity.

00:10:27.957 --> 00:10:30.293
CSB: You know, one thing
I've heard you say before

00:10:30.334 --> 00:10:34.255
is that genius can be created in seconds
and crap can be created over months.

00:10:34.297 --> 00:10:35.464
SB: Yeah.

00:10:35.464 --> 00:10:37.842
CSB: I think that's such a great line.

00:10:37.842 --> 00:10:39.886
SB: That's why you never charge hourly.

00:10:39.886 --> 00:10:41.053
CSB: That is so true.

00:10:41.095 --> 00:10:43.389
So know, anyone who hires consultants.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.517
So how does someone optimize their life

00:10:46.559 --> 00:10:50.313
so that they can avoid creating crap
over long amounts of time?

00:10:50.313 --> 00:10:51.689
SB: Maybe avoid distraction.

00:10:51.689 --> 00:10:55.860
I've always found
that when trying to create,

00:10:55.902 --> 00:10:58.863
not getting too distracted
by the thing that we all have

00:10:58.905 --> 00:11:00.072
in our pockets right now

00:11:00.114 --> 00:11:03.451
and constantly scrolling
other people's ideas,

00:11:03.492 --> 00:11:06.954
social media, the internet, Instagram,
it's a great sense of inspiration,

00:11:06.954 --> 00:11:09.540
but it can also create
a massive cloud of distraction

00:11:09.582 --> 00:11:13.878
that makes you lose sight
of your own vision.

00:11:13.878 --> 00:11:16.672
And so I think finding
that balance between the work

00:11:16.672 --> 00:11:18.674
and avoiding distraction is important.

00:11:19.842 --> 00:11:21.093
CSB: Yeah.

00:11:21.135 --> 00:11:23.137
You know, one thing that you're known for,

00:11:23.179 --> 00:11:24.347
beyond your shoes,

00:11:24.347 --> 00:11:27.475
is speaking about those
Easter-egg details in your work,

00:11:27.516 --> 00:11:28.726
is the boxes

00:11:28.726 --> 00:11:31.437
that you actually design
for the shoes, the shoe boxes.

00:11:31.479 --> 00:11:35.942
And so any sneakerhead I've spoken to
is like, oh, Salehe's boxes,

00:11:35.983 --> 00:11:39.820
like, "I love collecting his shoes
because they stack so beautifully."

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:43.574
So like, how much time do you spend
thinking about how the shoe will look

00:11:43.616 --> 00:11:45.326
on the foot versus in the box?

00:11:45.952 --> 00:11:49.413
SB: Well, I just think I often
go back to, you know,

00:11:49.455 --> 00:11:51.707
I'm pretty experienced in footwear design,

00:11:51.749 --> 00:11:54.001
but I have my master's
in footwear consumerism.

00:11:54.043 --> 00:11:57.088
So I just think of those moments
that resonated with me

00:11:57.129 --> 00:11:58.547
and that made me feel cool

00:11:58.547 --> 00:12:01.342
or more athletic or magical.

00:12:01.384 --> 00:12:04.178
And so I'm really just trying
to recreate those moments.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.472
I really think it's important
to take advantage

00:12:06.472 --> 00:12:08.683
of every area of real estate of a product,

00:12:08.683 --> 00:12:11.769
because as creatives, all we have
is that reveal moment, right?

00:12:11.769 --> 00:12:15.731
So when we reveal that product,
how does it make the audience feel?

00:12:16.107 --> 00:12:17.483
Do they like it immediately?

00:12:17.483 --> 00:12:19.026
Do they not like it immediately?

00:12:19.026 --> 00:12:22.488
And then they see it over and over again,
and then they start to like it.

00:12:22.488 --> 00:12:24.282
I love that relationship with product

00:12:24.323 --> 00:12:28.828
because it’s almost like “Steve Jobsian,”
if that’s the term.

00:12:29.537 --> 00:12:30.830
Because, you know, he said

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:34.083
the consumer doesn't know what they want
until you show it to them.

00:12:34.083 --> 00:12:35.876
So the Crocs Pollex being an example.

00:12:35.918 --> 00:12:38.796
Like, there are a lot of people
that that shoe upset at first

00:12:38.796 --> 00:12:42.341
and then slowly seeing them come around
to both that product specifically,

00:12:42.341 --> 00:12:45.386
or even the brand of Crocs
was really cool to witness

00:12:45.428 --> 00:12:48.973
because you almost were like,
changing minds with product.

00:12:50.016 --> 00:12:52.018
CSB: So this passion of yours runs deep.

00:12:52.059 --> 00:12:54.812
When you were nine years old,

00:12:54.854 --> 00:12:56.981
you made this --

00:12:57.023 --> 00:12:59.442
Air Jordan 11 out of clay.

00:12:59.483 --> 00:13:01.152
And I would love to know,

00:13:01.152 --> 00:13:03.988
do you still feel like
the nine-year-old version of yourself

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.032
when you do your work today?

00:13:06.073 --> 00:13:07.241
SB: Absolutely.

00:13:07.283 --> 00:13:09.535
I mean, if you love
what you do, it's not a job.

00:13:09.535 --> 00:13:12.330
This is pretty much what
I've wanted to do my entire life.

00:13:12.371 --> 00:13:15.249
So there’s a certain level of freedom.

00:13:15.291 --> 00:13:18.336
There's a certain level of nostalgia
that exists within my work.

00:13:18.336 --> 00:13:21.172
To quote Kanye West in one interview,

00:13:21.213 --> 00:13:24.008
he was saying that he felt
like he was creating most freely

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:26.010
when he felt like he was three years old.

00:13:26.052 --> 00:13:27.595
And that was a time in our lives

00:13:27.595 --> 00:13:30.097
when we didn’t have many
boundaries or restrictions.

00:13:30.139 --> 00:13:32.975
And our creative output was exactly
how we wanted it to be.

00:13:33.017 --> 00:13:37.813
And now we potentially
have employers or market rules

00:13:37.855 --> 00:13:39.565
or trends that we need to follow.

00:13:39.607 --> 00:13:41.901
And then those are all
ultimately like guardrails

00:13:41.942 --> 00:13:43.652
that prevent true creative freedom.

00:13:43.652 --> 00:13:47.323
So everything’s about balance
at the end of the day,

00:13:47.323 --> 00:13:50.201
but I still try to maintain
that freedom as much as possible.

00:13:50.242 --> 00:13:51.410
CSB: Awesome.

00:13:51.452 --> 00:13:52.995
Well, thank you so much, Salehe.

00:13:52.995 --> 00:13:54.246
SB: Thanks for having me.

00:13:54.246 --> 00:13:56.665
(Applause)

